Evidence 6.1 – Backup Creation and Checksum

Screenshot Filename: A6-01_Backup_Creation_and_Checksum.png

Description of Evidence:

This screenshot is intended to document the creation of a PostgreSQL logical backup using the pg_dump utility with the custom archive format.

The backup is saved as parkit_backup.dump. The file information and SHA-256 checksum provide additional details for identifying and checking the integrity of the backup file.

Purpose:

This evidence demonstrates the database backup process. Creating backups is important because it provides a recovery copy that can be used if the original database becomes unavailable or its data is lost or corrupted.

The checksum can help determine whether the backup file has changed since the checksum was generated. However, a checksum alone does not prove that the backup can be restored successfully.

Evidence 6.2 – Backup Archive Inspection

Screenshot Filename: A6-02_Backup_Archive_Inspection.png

Description of Evidence:

This screenshot is intended to document the inspection of the PostgreSQL custom-format backup archive using the pg_restore -l command.

The archive listing identifies the database objects included in the backup, such as tables, sequences, constraints, and other available objects.

Purpose:

The purpose of this evidence is to confirm that the backup archive can be read and that its contents can be inspected before restoration.

Inspecting the archive is an important step in validating the backup structure before attempting a recovery operation.

Evidence 6.3 – Restore and Database Verification

Screenshot Filename: A6-03_Restore_and_Database_Verification.png

Description of Evidence:

This screenshot is intended to document the restoration of the backup into a separate test database named grp_parkit_restore_test.

The restored database is inspected to determine whether its tables and records are available. The verification includes checking the tables and retrieving record counts from the vehicle, parking_zone, staff_user, payment, and transaction tables.

Purpose:

This evidence demonstrates the database restoration process and provides an initial check of the restored database contents.

Restoring into a separate test database is important because it allows the administrator to evaluate the backup without overwriting or modifying the original project database.

